Z||000000||Cheever2||07-24-2001||04:01 PM||||Did a search, found descriptions for taking apart a door, but doesn't help without photos.  Can someone help?<P>I have 3 people waiting for parts, for which I need to get into the drivers door.  We have the inside panel off, and am trying to get the outer off, but all I have left is a hundred rivets.  I can't drill them out because they have posts which stick out.  I tried hammer & chisel, but they won't budge.<P>I would also appreciate seeing how to remove the door entirely.<P>TIA<P>BTW, it's an 87 GT w/ PW/PL/PM, and the window glass is up, with no way to put it down.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cheever2 (edited 07-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Voytek||07-24-2001||06:30 PM||||Edit -double post.<p>[This message has been edited by Voytek (edited 07-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Voytek||07-24-2001||06:30 PM||||I'm not sure what you mean by 100 rivets.  If you mean the weird looking bolts(or nuts) with Torx heads that hold the outer pannel from the inside of the door, they're easy to remove.  They are coated with plastic.  Yes, most of them are stuck and the plastic strips.  What I did is take a utility knife and shave the plastic off the heads.  You'll find that underneath is a metal Torx head and once you dig out the remaining bits of plastic from the head, you can unscrew them quite easily.  You will also need to remove the trim on the door (from the inside) and the lock/handle.  I assume you've already removed everything from the inside.<P>Sorry, no pictures.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cheever2||07-24-2001||07:19 PM||||No, these look like aluminum pop-rivets.  I have the interior door panel off, and I think I got the various torx screws out, but there are a LOT of these little pop-rivets, and they can't be drilled, because they stick out (the drill bit has no indented place to get started into).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||starfighter007@msn.com||07-24-2001||08:08 PM||||I am really not sure of what you are talking about.but have you thought of useing a grinder to get at those rivets.gosh I wish we could be more helpful ,but I am not real familar with the inside of the doors.sorry||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||07-24-2001||10:06 PM||||The onyl pop rivets I can think of inside the door do NOT have to be removed to take the outer door skin off.<P>Torx screws around the edges, weird fasteners at the bottom, the mirror housing, and the outside trim strip with associated screws beneath, and the outer skin can come off.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mbramble||07-24-2001||10:50 PM||||Cheever,<P>I have no photos but here is some general info re taking the doors apart or off the car.<P>You already have the inner panels off.  <P>To remove the outer panels you have to:<P>Remove the mirrors. To do that, remove the inner dew strip.  It's held on by a long metal strip that runs the length of the top of the door.  You also need to remove the front tringular shaped rubber trim piece that fits under the inner dew wipe and is held on by 3 phillips screws.  You can then reach down with a 10mm wrench to remove the two nuts that hold the mirror on.  Unplug the miiror from it's power cord and remove it.<P>Then, on the outside of the door, remove the long molding strip that runs from the door handle to the front edge of the door. Lift up the door handle and you will see a black plastic "rivet" that holds the rear edge of the molding in place.  Use a screwdriver to get up under the molding and pry out the rivet.  There are 3-4 clips that hold the molding on.  Carefully pry up the molding from the back edge, if you are trying to save the clips.  It's an almost futile effort to try and save them but sometimes you can get them out whole.  On the front edge of the door, under where the molding is mounted is a clip that must be unscrewed.  Also, just forward of the door handle is another screw that needs to be removed.<P>On the very bottom of the door are 5-6 plastic rivets.  Just grab them with a pair of pliers and yank them out.<P>Then remove the torx headed bolts at the front and back inside edges of the door.  They look like they are plastic but are basically plastic covers over metal torx headed screws.  4 or 5 at the back edge of the door, about one less at the front of the door, I think.  <P>Pull the door skin back where the handle is.  You will see a couple of metal rods behind the skin that need to be unclipped.  One goes to the door lock, the other to the door latch.  <P>Then you can lift the skin off the door.  It is clipped over a channel at the top of the door.  Just lift up to remove it.  <P>To remove the rivets that hold the window motors, power lock assemblies in place -- take a punch and knock out the center post in the rivet.  Then you can drill the rivet out.<P>To remove the entire door from the car:<P>Romove the lower plasic trim piece inside the car that runs from front to back under the door opening.  Pull back the carpet on the "side walls".  Ummmm, if you see where the hinges are mounted on the outside of the car, pull back the carpet directly opposite where the hinges are....  There is a rubber boot for running wires between the car and the door.  When you pull back the carpet, you will see wiring that runs into the boot.  Disconnect the connectors there - one for power windows/locks, 1 for power mirrors, maybe one for the Door Ajar switch??.  Down below that opening in the sidewall is a smaller opening covered up with sticky goo and paper.  Pull the paper away and inside is one of the bolts for the door hinges.  I think it is 15mm.  Remove that bolt and try not to lose your socket and bolt down in that little hole!!  Then, from outside the car remove the other 3 hinge bolts (13mm I think).  The easiest way for me has been to remove the 10 mm bolts that hold the fender on.  Then remove the little 7mm screws around the lower edges of the fender.  You can then pull the fender back and get to the three remaining hinge bolts with the door still closed.  With it closed, you can remove the final bolt and hold the door in it's position.  After the last one is out, pull on the door handle to unlatch it and lift the whole door off the car.  It's heavy but more bulky.  It would be best if the window is down as you remove the door!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||tgowens||07-25-2001||12:41 PM||||well Chuck, mrbramble gave GREAT instructions and that is exactly how you do it......once you have the door off you can see the components inside. If you need to remove the lock actuators (for example) you will have to drill out the rivits holding the components to the door. Be careful if you are removing the window glass.......those rivits need to be drilled out carefully to prevent breaking the glass.||||reg||1
